# Controller Patterns

Controllers stay thin: strong params, auth, build a form, render. No business logic or SQL.

Use a compact class name (`class API::V1::User::RegistrationsController`) and call `User::Registration` directly.

```ruby
class API::V1::User::RegistrationsController < API::V1::BaseController
  skip_before_action :authenticate_user!

  def create
    registration = User::Registration.new(registration_params)

    if registration.save
      render_json_with_success(status: :created, message: I18n.t("user.registration.success"), data: { user: User::Serializer.new(registration.user) })
    else
      render_json_with_model_errors(registration)
    end
  end

  private

  def registration_params
    params.permit(:first_name, :last_name, :email, :password, :password_confirmation).to_h
  end
end
```

- Public endpoints: `skip_before_action :authenticate_user!`.
- Protected endpoints: inherit `authenticate_user!` and merge `user: current_user`.
- Session create: merge `request_metadata` (`ip_address`, `user_agent`) into params. On success, call `set_refresh_token_as_cookie` when `refresh_token` is present.
- Refresh: read `cookies.encrypted["refresh_token"]`; on success call `set_refresh_token_as_cookie`.
- Session revoke calls `current_user.sessions.active.revoke_all` and renders success. It has no form.
- Render via `render_json_with_success` / `render_json_with_error` / `render_json_with_model_errors` only.

Reference: `API::V1::User::RegistrationsController` (public), `API::V1::User::ProfilesController` (authenticated), `API::V1::User::AuthenticationsController` (session cookie).
